{% extends "layout.njk" %}
{% block title %}API — Pixelsmith{% endblock %}
{% block content %}
<h1 class="page-title">REST API</h1>
<p class="lede">
  Scriptable access to every tool the web pages use — images and PDFs alike.
  Nothing here talks to the outside world: the API is the same server, on the
  same machine, as the pages.
</p>

<h2 class="section-title">Getting in</h2>
<p>
  {% if openAccess %}
    This deployment is open, so no credentials are needed to create a job.
  {% else %}
    This deployment uses accounts, so sign in first and keep the session cookie.
    An unauthenticated call is answered with <code>401</code> and JSON.
  {% endif %}
</p>
<p>
  Identity still matters after that, because a job belongs to whoever created it
  and is nobody else's to read. Creating one answers with a
  <code>token</code>; send it back as <code>X-Job-Token</code> on the calls that
  read the job, its progress or its files. A browser needs no token, having been
  given a cookie, but a script has no cookie jar — and without either, reading
  your own job is answered <code>404</code> like anyone else's.
</p>
<p class="hint">
  The token is the job's only protection, so treat it as one: it goes in a
  header rather than the URL, because URLs end up in logs and history.
  <code>GET /api/jobs/{id}/events</code> is the exception — a browser's
  <code>EventSource</code> cannot set headers, so it relies on the cookie. From
  a script, poll the status instead.
</p>
<p class="hint">
  There is no API-key mechanism yet. The table exists in the database but
  nothing issues or verifies keys — worth knowing before you write a client
  around it. Per-job tokens are not a substitute: they authorise one job, not a
  caller.
</p>

<h2 class="section-title">Endpoints</h2>
<table class="table">
  <thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Path</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr data-api-endpoint="GET /healthz">
      <td><code>GET</code></td><td><code>/healthz</code></td>
      <td>Liveness, and which queue driver is active.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr data-api-endpoint="GET /api/tools">
      <td><code>GET</code></td><td><code>/api/tools</code></td>
      <td>Every tool: family, what it accepts, and its settings.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr data-api-endpoint="GET /api/tools/{id}">
      <td><code>GET</code></td><td><code>/api/tools/{id}</code></td>
      <td>One tool's settings, in detail.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr data-api-endpoint="POST /api/jobs">
      <td><code>POST</code></td><td><code>/api/jobs</code></td>
      <td>Create a job. Multipart. Answers <code>202</code> with the job id.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr data-api-endpoint="GET /api/jobs/{id}">
      <td><code>GET</code></td><td><code>/api/jobs/{id}</code></td>
      <td>Status, and the outputs once it is done.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr data-api-endpoint="GET /api/jobs/{id}/events">
      <td><code>GET</code></td><td><code>/api/jobs/{id}/events</code></td>
      <td>Progress as server-sent events, if you would rather not poll.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr data-api-endpoint="GET /jobs/{id}/files/{fileId}">
      <td><code>GET</code></td><td><code>/jobs/{id}/files/{fileId}</code></td>
      <td>Download one result. The ids come from the status response.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr data-api-endpoint="GET /jobs/{id}/download">
      <td><code>GET</code></td><td><code>/jobs/{id}/download</code></td>
      <td>Download every result as a zip.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2 class="section-title">Creating a job</h2>
<p>
  One <code>multipart/form-data</code> request to <code>/api/jobs</code>:
</p>
<table class="table">
  <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>tool</code></td>
      <td>Which tool to run, by id. Required.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>files</code></td>
      <td>
        The documents or pictures to work on — repeat the field for several.
        Up to {{ maxFiles }} per job, {{ maxUploadMb }} MB each. Omit it for a
        tool that builds its result from settings alone.
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>any setting</td>
      <td>
        A field per setting, named as <code>/api/tools/{id}</code> reports it.
        Unrecognised fields are ignored; invalid ones are refused with a reason.
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>a named file field</td>
      <td>
        Supporting files go in the field the tool names for them —
        <code>signatureFile</code> for signing, <code>markFile</code> for an
        image watermark. They are not treated as inputs.
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>fromJob</code></td>
      <td>
        A finished job's id, to carry its results straight in instead of
        downloading and re-uploading them.
      </td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>No CSRF token is involved: that belongs to the browser form, which has a page to take one from.</p>

<h2 class="section-title">When something is wrong</h2>
<p>
  Every failure answers with JSON shaped
  <code>{"error": {"code", "message"}}</code>. The message is meant to be shown
  to a person as it is.
</p>
<table class="table">
  <thead><tr><th>Status</th><th>Means</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code>400</code></td><td>A setting is invalid, or a required file is missing.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>401</code></td><td>Credentials are needed on this deployment.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>404</code></td><td>No such tool, or no such job for you.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>413</code></td><td>Too large, or too many files.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>415</code></td><td>The file is not a sort this tool reads.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2 class="section-title">An example, start to finish</h2>
<pre class="code"><code>HOST=http://localhost:8080

# Create the job. Keep the id and the token it answers with.
CREATED=$(curl -sS -X POST $HOST/api/jobs \
  -F "tool=merge-pdf" \
  -F "files=@first.pdf" -F "files=@second.pdf" \
  -F "filename=joined")
JOB=$(echo "$CREATED" | jq -r .id)
TOKEN=$(echo "$CREATED" | jq -r .token)

# Wait for it. The token is what makes this readable without a cookie jar.
until curl -sS -H "X-Job-Token: $TOKEN" "$HOST/api/jobs/$JOB" \
  | jq -e '.status == "done" or .status == "failed"' >/dev/null; do sleep 1; done

# Take the result.
URL=$(curl -sS -H "X-Job-Token: $TOKEN" "$HOST/api/jobs/$JOB" | jq -r '.outputs[0].url')
curl -sS -H "X-Job-Token: $TOKEN" "$HOST$URL" -o joined.pdf</code></pre>

<h2 class="section-title">Every tool</h2>
{% for family in families %}
  <h3 class="section-subtitle">{{ family.title }}</h3>
  <ul class="plain-list">
    {% for group in family.groups %}{% for t in group.tools %}
      <li data-api-tool="{{ t.id }}">
        <code>{{ t.id }}</code> — {{ t.title }}
        <a class="muted" href="/api/tools/{{ t.id }}">settings</a>
      </li>
    {% endfor %}{% endfor %}
  </ul>
{% endfor %}
{% endblock %}
